    content: `A diode is a two-terminal semiconductor component that allows current to flow in ONE direction only and blocks it in the other — it is the electrical equivalent of a one-way valve (check valve). Its two terminals are the ANODE (+) and the CATHODE (−, marked with a band on the body). HOW IT WORKS: a diode is a single P–N JUNCTION. When the anode is made more positive than the cathode by more than the "forward voltage" (about 0.7 V for a silicon diode, ~0.3 V for germanium, ~0.2 V for Schottky, 1.8–3.3 V for an LED), the junction's depletion region collapses and the diode conducts — "forward biased" — dropping only that roughly constant small voltage regardless of current. With the polarity reversed ("reverse biased"), the depletion region widens and only a tiny leakage current flows — until the reverse voltage exceeds the diode's breakdown (peak inverse voltage) rating, beyond which it conducts hard in reverse and (for an ordinary diode) is usually destroyed. USES: rectification (converting AC to DC — see rectifier); reverse-polarity protection; "freewheel"/flyback clamping across inductive loads like relay coils and motors, so the inductor's collapsing field doesn't spike and damage a transistor; voltage clamping and logic gating; radio signal demodulation (detecting the envelope). SPECIAL TYPES: the ZENER diode is designed to be run in reverse breakdown at a precise voltage and is used as a cheap voltage reference/regulator; the SCHOTTKY diode has a low forward drop and switches very fast (used in power supplies); the LED (light-emitting diode) emits light when forward-conducting; the PHOTODIODE and solar cell generate current from light. (A plain diode does not itself produce light — that answer confused it with an LED.)`,
