    content: `An LED (light-emitting diode) is a diode built from a direct-band-gap semiconductor (e.g. gallium arsenide phosphide, indium gallium nitride) that EMITS LIGHT when forward current flows: electrons injected across the p–n junction recombine with holes and release the energy difference as photons. The colour is set by the semiconductor's band gap — infrared, red, amber, green, blue, violet, or UV; "white" LEDs are usually a blue LED with a yellow phosphor coating. Like any diode it conducts only one way (anode +, cathode −, the shorter lead / flat side). WHY A SERIES RESISTOR IS NEEDED: an LED is a non-linear device with a roughly FIXED forward voltage drop (Vf ≈ 1.8–2.2 V red, ~2.1 V yellow/green, ~3.0–3.4 V blue/white). Above that voltage its current rises almost vertically with voltage — the LED does essentially nothing to limit its own current. Connected directly across a supply whose voltage exceeds Vf, it would draw a huge, uncontrolled current and destroy itself (or the source) within moments, and tiny variations between LEDs or with temperature would make the current wildly unpredictable. A resistor in series absorbs the surplus voltage and, being linear, sets a stable current. SIZING IT: choose a target current I (typically 5–20 mA for indicator LEDs), then R = (Vsupply − Vf) / I. Example: 5 V supply, red LED Vf = 2 V, want 15 mA → R = (5 − 2)/0.015 = 200 Ω (use the next standard value, 220 Ω). Also check the resistor's power: P = (Vsupply − Vf)·I = 3 V × 0.015 A ≈ 45 mW, so a ¼ W resistor is fine. (For running many LEDs or high-power LEDs efficiently, a constant-current driver is used instead of a resistor.)`,
