    content: `A rectifier is an electrical circuit that converts ALTERNATING CURRENT (AC — which periodically reverses direction) into DIRECT CURRENT (DC — which flows one way). It is the opposite function to an inverter, and it is the first power-conversion stage inside almost every device that runs from the mains but needs DC internally (phone chargers, laptop bricks, TVs, LED drivers). Rectifiers are built from DIODES, which conduct in only one direction. (It is not a type of guitar amplifier, and not specifically the 1930s selenium component — those were hallucinated web results.) TYPES: (1) HALF-WAVE — a single diode in series; it passes the positive half-cycles of the AC and blocks the negative ones. Simple but wastes half the waveform and gives a very bumpy output. (2) FULL-WAVE, centre-tapped — two diodes and a centre-tapped transformer; both halves of the AC are used, the negative half being "flipped" positive. (3) FULL-WAVE BRIDGE — four diodes arranged in a diamond; uses both half-cycles, needs no centre tap, and is the most common design. SMOOTHING: the rectifier's output is still a series of DC humps ("pulsating DC"), so a large "reservoir" / "filter" capacitor is connected across it — it charges up to each peak and supplies the load from its stored charge between peaks, leaving only a small "ripple" voltage. A voltage REGULATOR (linear like a 7805, or a switching regulator) after that produces a clean, constant DC rail. Transformer → bridge rectifier → reservoir capacitor → regulator is the classic linear DC power supply. Modern chargers instead rectify the mains directly and use a high-frequency switch-mode converter for efficiency and small size.`,
