    category: 'Electronics',
    keywords: [
      'what is a pn junction depletion region built in potential forward reverse bias',
      'a p n junction is the boundary formed inside a single semiconductor crystal where a p type region hole rich meets an n type region electron rich it is the fundamental structure behind diodes transistors solar cells and leds',
      'right at the junction free electrons from the n side diffuse across and fill holes on the p side leaving behind a thin zone with no mobile charge carriers the depletion region the exposed fixed ion charges create a built in electric field and a built in potential about 0 7 v in silicon that opposes further diffusion',
      'forward bias applying plus to p and minus to n pushes carriers toward the junction shrinks the depletion region and once the applied voltage overcomes the built in potential current flows freely reverse bias widens the depletion region and blocks current except a tiny leakage until breakdown',
    ],
    content: `A P–N JUNCTION is the metallurgical boundary, formed WITHIN a single continuous semiconductor crystal, where a P-TYPE region (doped to have an excess of mobile positive "holes") meets an N-TYPE region (doped to have an excess of mobile electrons). It is the fundamental structure from which diodes, bipolar transistors, solar cells, LEDs, and photodiodes are built. WHAT HAPPENS AT THE JUNCTION: as soon as the two regions are joined, mobile electrons near the boundary on the n-side diffuse across and recombine with holes on the p-side (and vice versa). This leaves behind, in a thin strip straddling the junction, only the FIXED, ionised dopant atoms — positively charged donor ions on the n-side, negatively charged acceptor ions on the p-side — and no mobile carriers. That carrier-free strip is the DEPLETION REGION (or space-charge region). The exposed fixed charges set up an internal electric field pointing from n to p, and a corresponding "BUILT-IN POTENTIAL" (≈ 0.7 V for silicon, ≈ 0.3 V for germanium) — a potential hill that opposes and eventually halts further diffusion, reaching equilibrium. BIASING: apply an external voltage with + to the p-side and − to the n-side ("FORWARD BIAS"): this opposes the built-in field, pushes carriers toward the junction, narrows the depletion region, and once the applied voltage exceeds the built-in potential, current flows readily. Apply the opposite polarity ("REVERSE BIAS"): the external field adds to the built-in field, pulls carriers away, WIDENS the depletion region, and only a minute leakage current flows — until the reverse voltage is large enough to cause avalanche or Zener breakdown. This asymmetric behaviour is exactly what makes a single p–n junction act as a diode (one-way valve).`,
